course: dictionary
lesson: 4653
title: Formal words and old-fashioned language
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to formal writing, such as exam papers, history books and old stories. Hitherto is one of them. It means until now, or up to this point. Writers use it to show that something was true before a change happened.
explain: The Rat looks at «the great ring of Downs» to the south, «his simple horizon hitherto, his Mountains of the Moon, his limit». Until now, those hills were the edge of his world. The Mountains of the Moon is an old name for faraway mountains, so the story is showing how far the Rat has never been.
explain: Look for a change in the sentence. «Hitherto incredulous» means until now not believing, so the person has just started to believe something. Put «until now» in place of hitherto. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
fact: Hitherto means until now, or up to this point.
fact: Hitherto often comes before a word like unknown or unseen: a hitherto unseen creature is one nobody had seen until now.
fact: «Hitherto incredulous» means until now not believing.
fact: Hitherto is formal and old-fashioned, so you will mostly meet it in writing, not in everyday talk.
fact: The swap test works well: if «until now» fits in the sentence, hitherto must mean until now.
exercise: The Rat looks at «the great ring of Downs» to the south, «his simple horizon hitherto». What does hitherto mean here? | Towards the sea | Far away and never reached | *Until now | Tomorrow
exercise: A scientist found a hitherto unknown beetle. What does hitherto tell us? | It is still hidden today | It was found very quickly | It was found after a long search | *Nobody had known about it until then
exercise: Ella had hitherto never tasted curry. What does hitherto mean? | She tasted curry once and never again | *She had never tasted curry until now | She tasted curry every day | She will taste curry tomorrow
test: Hitherto is mostly found in which kind of writing? | Text messages between friends | Shouting in the playground | *Formal writing, exam papers and old stories | Labels on cereal boxes
test: The hitherto quiet village was suddenly busy. What does this mean? | The village would be quiet tomorrow | The village was never quiet | The village was quiet only at night | *Until then the village had been quiet
exam: In the phrase «hitherto unseen», what does hitherto mean? | *Until now, so nobody had seen it before this | Close by | Very soon | Never again
